  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the development of dynamic motions and molecular machines in metal–organic frameworks over time, arranged along a vertical timeline from 2008 to 2021. It compares three kinds of behavior, color-coded as random motions, switches, and machines.\n\nEarly examples are mostly non-directional motions inside MOF cages, including rotational and linear movements in systems such as MOF-5, MOF-1001, MOF-1030, and UWDM materials. These are illustrated with red circular arrows and back-and-forth arrows, emphasizing that the components move but without a preferred direction. Around the middle of the timeline, the figure introduces artificial molecular switches, including rotaxane- and catenane-based designs, showing more controlled but still localized transformations.\n\nLater entries show a clear progression toward directional function. A 2019 Zn-MOF is highlighted for unidirectional rotational motion, and by 2021 a Zr-MOF is shown with unidirectional linear movement. The enlarged schematic at the upper right appears to depict a redox-driven process that repeatedly moves molecular units along linear chains, suggesting coordinated, machine-like transport. Overall, the figure conveys a shift from simple random dynamics in porous frameworks to increasingly sophisticated, directional molecular machines embedded in MOFs.",
